# DESCRIPTION

k1-pcie-phy drives the SpacemiT K1's three PCIe PHYs: port A's, the combo PHY at `0xc0b10000`, which PCIe 0 shares with USB 3, and those of ports B and C, at `0xc0c10000` and `0xc0d10000`, two lanes each with a PLL they share.
devmgr(8) starts one for each by its record, after its providers, and before the devices that link to it:

```text
driver k1-pcie-phy
class phy
match dt spacemit,k1-combo-phy
match dt spacemit,k1-pcie-phy
```

Each releases its own reset (its `phy` reset, the port's global one) and sets its `default` pins when it starts.

Only the combo PHY can calibrate the termination of a PHY's receiver and transmitter, and the PHYs of ports B and C take its result: devmgr gives each of them the combo PHY's tree by a link the binding implies (`phy calibration` in its `/dev/dt/links`).
The combo PHY's driver calibrates when it starts: it releases port A's hold on the PHY's reset through k1-syscon(3) (`pcie 0x3cc hold-phy off`), and if firmware has calibrated (the result register's done bit), takes that result.
Otherwise it gives the combo PHY to PCIe (`combo pcie`), turns PCIe 0's `dbi`, `mstr` and `slv` clocks on and releases their resets, waits up to 500 ms for the hardware to finish, and puts the clocks and resets back.

A port's PHY is turned on by its host, k1-pcie(3), between PERST#'s assertion and release.
Turning it on sets each lane's termination from the calibration, its reference clock to the board's 24 MHz crystal (recalibrating the lane's RC), and the reference clock driven to the slot; then, on lane 0, the PLL's input to 24 MHz with no spread spectrum and its 100 MHz output on, each lane marked set up, and a wait of up to 500 ms for the PLL to lock.
Its lanes are its node's `num-lanes`, 1 or 2, else 2.
The registers, bits and order are the hardware's, as Linux's driver gives them.

The combo PHY can be USB 3's instead, the SuperSpeed PHY of the DWC3 that xhci(3) drives, whose `phys` name it with the cell 4 (PHY_TYPE_USB3).
Turning it on for USB 3 gives it to USB through k1-syscon(3) (`combo usb`), writes its PLL's and receiver's setup, waits up to 500 ms for the PLL to lock, and sets the receiver's LFPS threshold; the values are the vendor's.
Its calibration, already taken, stays served to ports B and C.

# FILES

The tree is served in devmgr(8)'s, at `drivers/k1-pcie-phy-dt-phy@<address>`, and at `/dev/provider/<service>` in the drivers of the devices that link to it.

: `calibration`
  The combo PHY's, read only: `rx <n> tx <n>`, the termination codes its calibration gave (0 to 15), or empty if it failed.
: `ctl`
  A command a line; it reads empty.
  A port's PHY takes `on`; the combo PHY takes `on 4`, USB 3's mode.
  Another line fails the write as invalid; so does `on` when the combo PHY has no calibration, `on 4` when k1-syscon(3) refuses `combo usb`, and either when the PLL doesn't lock in 500 ms, and the driver logs which.
: `status`
  Read only.
  `combo`, `combo, USB 3, PLL locked|unlocked` once given to USB 3, or `port, <n> lanes, PLL locked|unlocked`, the PLL as its register reads now; then `termination rx <n> tx <n>` (`-` before it has one); then `commands <n>, failed <n>`, and `last failure: <command>: <why>`.

